commons gatherings

---
How many places a gathering has.

At least one, or nobody could come; at most 500, the most Turn up is built
for. The rule is part of the type, so a `Capacity` that exists is already
in range.
---
type Capacity = Int where InRange(1, 500)

--- A gathering's title: something to read, short enough for a list.
type Title = String where MinLength(3) && MaxLength(80)

---
Something people sign up for: a talk, a workshop, a club night.

In prose this is an *event*. The code calls it a `Gathering`, because Bynk
has events of its own: messages between contexts, which Turn up meets in
part 6.
---
type Gathering = {
	title: Title,
	capacity: Capacity,
	startsAt: Instant,
}

---
A gathering from unchecked values, such as a form or a request.

Each refined field goes through its `.of`, which returns a `Result`; `?`
hands back the first rule that fails.
---
fn gathering(
	title: String,
	capacity: Int,
	startsAt: Instant
) -> Result[Gathering, ValidationError] {
	let t = Title.of(title)?
	let c = Capacity.of(capacity)?
	Ok(Gathering { title: t, capacity: c, startsAt: startsAt })
}

---
The capacity a room in the starter venue holds.

`12` is a literal in a `Capacity` position, so the compiler checks it against
`InRange(1, 500)` when it compiles: no `.of`, no `Result`. Writing `0` or
`501` here would be a compile error, not a test failure.
---
fn smallRoom() -> Capacity {
	12
}
